@@ -495,6 +495,29 @@ public class KeyguardViewMediator implements CoreStartable,
 
     private final Object mDismissToken = new Object();
 
+    /**
+     * SovereignOS: set in handleBeforeUserSwitching when the incoming switch holds a still-valid
+     * decoy-switch authorization, by redeeming that mint's bouncer-skip grant. It drives three
+     * things, in order: the bouncer is left up across the switch instead of being collapsed into
+     * the incoming user's lock screen (handleBeforeUserSwitching), the automatic bouncer re-arm is
+     * skipped and the dismissal is requested immediately (handleUserSwitchComplete), and the
+     * canned unlock animation is suppressed (KeyguardUnlockAnimationController#
+     * playCannedUnlockAnimation, which consumes it via consumeAuthorizedSwitchDismiss).
+     *
+     * The first of those is what makes the switch look like an ordinary unlock. Collapsing the
+     * bouncer drives PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> LOCKSCREEN, so the incoming user's lock screen animates
+     * into view and then has to be animated away again by a second LOCKSCREEN -> GONE transition
+     * -- 471ms plus 416ms, measured on hardware, and that pair is what reads as the device swiping
+     * itself up. Leaving the bouncer up keeps the state machine on PRIMARY_BOUNCER -> GONE, the
+     * single-step path an ordinary unlock takes.
+     *
+     * Read and written only on the mHandler/mediator thread. Reassigned on every switch, so a
+     * superseded one cannot leave a stale mark behind. It gates presentation only -- it never
+     * decides whether a dismissal is permitted, which UserController's grants settle before it is
+     * ever set, and it can only ever be set by redeeming a real single-use grant.
+     */
+    private boolean mAuthorizedSwitchDismissPending;
+
     // last known state of the cellular connection
     private final String mPhoneState = TelephonyManager.EXTRA_STATE_IDLE;
 
